or a stay in place.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:56.990
Like here in California we
haven't had a full shutdown,

00:12:56.990 --> 00:12:58.980
we're allowed to go outside,

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.530
but they are enforcing a stay in place.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.650
So there's only some very basic
functions that are in place.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:06.580
China, in Wuhan, did a full shutdown,

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:09.703
and so they probably saw pretty good,

00:13:10.568 --> 00:13:12.340
and it still took them two to four weeks

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:15.430
to get to a leveling off period.

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:18.210
So in places like California
where it's not as full

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:22.840
of a shutdown, I hope that
can happen in that timeframe,

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:25.169
but even if you imagine that,

00:13:25.169 --> 00:13:27.390
which I think would be
on the more optimistic

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:29.210
sides of things, it's
not that all of a sudden

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:32.300
people are gonna open up all
of the institutions at peak,

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:34.376
'cause the worst thing you want to do is

00:13:34.376 --> 00:13:35.926
that things start to level off

00:13:35.926 --> 00:13:36.975
and then you open everything up

00:13:36.975 --> 00:13:37.808
and then things go back exponential.

00:13:38.817 --> 00:13:42.560
So my best guess is that it's
going to be several weeks,

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:44.540
and you can imagine the school year,

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:45.800
at least in the Northern Hemisphere

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:48.440
we have about two months
left, 2 1/2 months left.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:52.230
So I think it's possible that this goes

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:53.990
through the end of the school year.

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.093
Not an expert though.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.000
Let's see.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.550
So Isaiah Hong says, hi Sal, hi Sal Khan,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.610
a fellow college student here.

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:05.990
Maybe I don't look so old to Isaiah,

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:07.540
he says fellow college student,

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:11.160
how will I know myself if I
have mastered the material

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.610
I've been studying, any
tips or key indicators

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.910
to know if I have demonstrated mastery?

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:20.220
Well, one of the key tenants
for those of y'all listening

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:22.460
and don't know about
mastery and mastery learning

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:24.670
at Khan Academy is this notion of mastery,

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:26.810
that in traditional academics oftentimes

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.720
you're exposed to the material

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.960
and maybe you, and then the class

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.430
might move on to the next subject.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:34.930
And then that gap, and
maybe it's in something

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:37.440
pretty fundamental, it
might be in basic exponents.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.739
Now all of a sudden
you go to the next unit

00:14:39.739 --> 00:14:40.870
that's on negative
exponents, on logarithms

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:42.540
and you're expected to understand it.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:45.760
And so Isaiah, I think there's
two ways to think about it.

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.490
There's kind of the
general term of mastery

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:49.730
that people use in everyday language,

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.190
and then the, I guess you'd
say more academic version

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:54.501
that we use called mastery learning

00:14:54.501 --> 00:14:56.527
which is making sure that you
can learn at your own pace

00:14:56.527 --> 00:14:58.283
and fill in all of your gaps.

00:14:58.283 --> 00:14:59.758
So to answer your question as to

00:14:59.758 --> 00:15:00.853
how do you know that you've mastered it,

00:15:00.853 --> 00:15:04.040
I think the question maybe is mastered

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.800
at a certain degree of knowledge.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:08.630
And that's why when you go, for example,

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:10.770
on math or in many of the
sciences on Khan Academy

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:12.910
we have what we call
these mastery mechanics.

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:17.020
So that for every skill you can,

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:20.070
you get to familiar,
proficient, or mastered

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.950
depending on, have you done
it on kind of skill-focused

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:25.790
practice, have you done it in kind of

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:29.000
a context switching
environment on our unit tests

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.250
or our course challenges?

00:15:31.234 --> 00:15:33.210
So if you're on Khan Academy
and if you're in a course

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:36.080
and you get full mastery
by doing the unit tests,

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.130
the master challenges,
the course challenges.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:41.660
On that course challenge, you
can get a 90-plus percent,

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:44.280
you will have mastered that content,

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:46.450
at least in the modalities
that Khan Academy

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:47.900
is presenting them to you.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:50.010
If you want to go even
deeper in a subject,

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:51.590
especially in something like math,

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.320
then I expect, then I would go after

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:57.110
some of the more multi-part questions,

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.540
even for some of the
things like our AP prep

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.010
we have videos that go
into deeper questions

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.010
that you really can't
do in a multiple choice,

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.020
in a free response modality.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:09.710
And there pause the video
before I work through it

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.480
or one of our video
creators work through it

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.220
and try to work through it on your own.

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.847
And I think if you're seeing
that, you'll have mastered it.

00:16:15.847 --> 00:16:17.380
I think the next level may
be even above a mastery,

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:20.187
and this goes to a previous
question that someone asked is,

00:16:20.187 --> 00:16:22.350
teach the material to other people.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:24.730
That's really the best way to
ensure that you can master it.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:26.430
Because if you can really
distill it in simple ways

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:29.380
and teach it to others, then
I think you know the material.

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:35.470
Okay, other question?

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:41.160
Yeah, and my apologies,
I've gotten multiple points

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.610
of feedback now that the
internet connection here

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:44.860
is not good.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:46.990
So I'm going to try to fix this overnight

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:51.800
and have a better internet
connection for everyone tomorrow.

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:56.370
So let's see, other questions.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.810
Kim McDonald, this is a parent, and says,

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:01.040
is there an easy way to
learn how to make assignments

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:02.053
for my kids?

00:17:03.032 --> 00:17:05.660
I'm new here and I want
them working every day.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:08.230
And so the simple answer is yes,

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:10.140
you can make assignments on Khan Academy.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.580
You can actually register
using the teacher tools

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:16.120
as a parent and then you
could either add your child on

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:18.680
as one of your students
or there's a little code

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:19.770
and then they can add you on

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:21.530
as their coach or their teacher.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:23.729
And then once you're a teacher,

00:17:23.729 --> 00:17:25.201
if you go to any piece of content

00:17:25.201 --> 00:17:26.839
you can actually see a little thing

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:28.070
that shows up at the top
that says you can assign it

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:30.180
and you'll only have one student

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.988
or however many children you have.

00:17:31.988 --> 00:17:33.719
So, yes, you can assign content,

00:17:33.719 --> 00:17:35.680
but I also encourage, especially
in subjects like math,

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:38.510
let your student learn
at his or her own pace

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:41.230
so that they can master
concepts at their time and pace

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.000
and that for you to support them,

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:44.390
answer any questions they might have

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:46.913
and provide some motivation.

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:51.390
See, other questions.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:54.110
So someone asks, hey Sal, it's really hard

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:56.530
for me to concentrate at home, any advice

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:58.190
for finding motivation when you don't

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:00.023
have any outside pressure?

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.301
This is a very good question,

00:18:03.301 --> 00:18:05.652
and I think this is
something that we all face,

00:18:05.652 --> 00:18:07.920
especially now that most
of us, or all of us,

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:09.970
are going to be working from home.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:11.393
But it's an important skill,

00:18:12.243 --> 00:18:13.896
because especially once
you're out of school,

00:18:13.896 --> 00:18:16.082
you know, my day, I don't
have someone saying,

00:18:16.082 --> 00:18:16.915
hey, do this for the next hour

00:18:17.957 --> 00:18:18.790
then do that for the next
hour, sometimes I do.

00:18:20.211 --> 00:18:22.590
But the way to try to
do it is I give myself

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:24.970
a list of things that I hope
to accomplish in a given day

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:28.040
or in a given week and
what I do is as I'm able

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:30.160
to hopefully accomplish some of them,

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:31.620
and some of them are longer ranging things

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:33.749
that can't be done in an hour,

00:18:33.749 --> 00:18:35.010
but some of them are things
that can be done that hour.

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:37.940
And it could be in progress
to something bigger.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:41.010
And what I tell myself is if
I can knock this stuff out,

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:43.740
then I have earned a right to, whatever,

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.730
take a break, go for
a walk, whatever else.

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:48.100
I find that pretty motivating.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:51.190
I think it's also motivating
just to remind yourself,

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:54.003
this is your opportunity to
really invest in yourself.

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:59.150
A lot of times I think students
maybe have the right mindset

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:01.650
to school where they're kind of just like,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:04.633
oh, I gotta be here, what do I have to do?

00:19:04.633 --> 00:19:08.180
But you have to realize school
is massive investment in you,

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:11.850
in your capabilities,
and now that you're home,

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.240
and we'll try to support
you as best as we can

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.230
and try to come up with more
mechanisms to support you,

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:20.640
but the simple answer
is this is your chance

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:24.200
to build that other muscle,
not just the algebra

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.540
or the reading
comprehension or the science

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:30.000
but build the muscle of
actually learning how to learn

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.700
and learning to self motivate here,

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:36.360
which I think is in some ways
the most valuable meta skill

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:37.963
you can have in life.

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:40.493
Okay.

00:19:40.493 --> 00:19:43.070
I see someone asks, I
just missed a question,

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:44.073
that was an interesting one.

00:19:45.475 --> 00:19:50.475
It says, what is, let me
get back on the questions

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:52.063
from the team.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:55.830
Let's see, I have another question,

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:59.580
tips on how you make videos for students.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:01.620
So I wish I could show
you my office right now,

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.500
which I'm not at because
of the stay at home orders

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:06.970
that we have here in Northern California.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.870
But my setup is pretty simple.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:12.740
I have a basic laptop,
actually this is the laptop

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:15.750
that I've recorded many
Khan Academy videos on.

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.280
I have a $150 Samson microphone.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:20.820
You could probably find it online

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:23.110
or at a local electronics store.

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:25.214
Doesn't have to be that one,

00:20:25.214 --> 00:20:26.047
just something that sounds decent.

00:20:26.047 --> 00:20:28.230
It's not super, all out
professional microphone.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:32.190
And I use a Wacom tablet to draw on,

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:35.240
so it's a tablet that you can write on

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.560
and it takes a little
bit of getting used to

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:38.676
but I've been doing it for 10 years now

00:20:38.676 --> 00:20:40.800
so I'm very used to it, 14 years now.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:44.080
On top of that, what I
use is a drawing program.

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.250
In the old days, I used Microsoft Paint,

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:48.010
then I ended up using other tools.

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:52.580
Now I use SketchBook
Pro as the art program

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:56.150
and then I use Camtasia
Studio to do screen capture

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:59.150
while I'm drawing on the art program.

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:01.520
So that is how I make the videos,

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:03.050
kind of the meta tips I would have

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:05.800
if you want to make videos
is just get started.

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:08.070
I think it's very easy to overplan

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:09.850
or to convince yourself you're not ready,

00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:11.970
and there's nothing
like kind of jumping in

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:13.650
and getting started,
especially if your audience

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:15.130
is for people you know and you care about.

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:16.680
I think it was a blessing for me

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.760
that Khan Academy started with my cousins.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:20.864
It allowed me to say, all
right, it's my cousins,

00:21:20.864 --> 00:21:24.751
I'm just gonna press record
and see what happens.

00:21:24.751 --> 00:21:27.080
But I think that allowed the videos

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:28.620
to be more conversational,

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:31.120
to be a little bit more comfortable.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:32.880
You don't want to be super improvised,

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.850
but a little bit of improvisation is nice,

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:37.690
people can recognize that
it's not being constructed

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:40.620
by some corporation
with large focus groups,

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.860
et cetera, et cetera, it's one human being

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:44.960
trying to communicate
with another human being.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.340
So I really encourage you,
I can't tell you how many,

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:49.560
I had friends that I tried
to recruit to make videos

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.681
in the early days of Khan Academy

00:21:51.681 --> 00:21:53.450
and they're brilliant people
who know how to explain things,

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:55.140
passionate about their subject.

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:57.260
If I talked to them at
a coffee shop about it,

00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:59.306
I'm like, oh, can I just record you?

00:21:59.306 --> 00:22:00.850
'Cause the way you just
explained quantum physics

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:03.010
is inspiring and really
easy to understand.

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:05.200
Then as soon as I try
to make 'em do a video,

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:06.910
they would get all tight
and they'd be like,

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:08.670
okay, the next step is, and it's like,

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:09.733
what happened to you?

00:22:10.765 --> 00:22:12.622
And I think sometimes you
can get into your head

00:22:12.622 --> 00:22:14.528
and you're so concerned
what other people will think

00:22:14.528 --> 00:22:15.740
that everyone gravitates to a safe space

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:17.270
and they try not to be themselves

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:18.970
and they try to be what they think

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.590
other people think an
expert should sound like.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:24.320
So I wouldn't do that, I
would try to be yourself.

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:27.600
Have your personality out
there and be vulnerable

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:28.963
with your students.

00:22:28.963 --> 00:22:32.390
But obviously feel very
comfortable with the material

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:35.263
and I think it'll come out just great.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:41.480
Let's see, Jessica Swanepoel
says, not a question,

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:43.530
but want to say my daughter
followed the schedule yesterday

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:45.116
and loved it.

00:22:45.116 --> 00:22:46.530
She was very excited to get
started again this morning.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:48.530
Awesome, that's great news to hear

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:50.930
'cause we put the schedules
out over the weekend

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:55.518
and it's good to get feedback,
and positive feedback

00:22:55.518 --> 00:22:56.773
helps sometimes.

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:58.900
But negative feedback is good too,

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:02.270
so everyone should be encouraged
to give negative feedback,

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:05.020
critical feedback, because that
was our first draft schedule

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:08.100
for students and we hope to
refine it as we learn more.

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:09.750
So also give us feedback
and just say, hey,

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.121
that period was a little bit too long

00:23:12.121 --> 00:23:14.590
for students of that age or
actually she wanted to do more

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:16.350
but the schedule said
move on to the next thing

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:17.870
or that break was a little bit too long

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:20.540
or we have ideas for other
parts of the schedule,

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:23.610
let us know, we would
love to make that schedule

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.310
better and better.

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:27.980
Let's see, Heather Johnson
says, is Khan Academy free?

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:29.590
That's a question I love to answer.

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:32.430
Khan Academy is free, that's why we exist.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:34.200
We are a not-for-profit organization

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:36.660
with a mission of providing
a free world class education

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:37.900
for anyone anywhere.

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:41.438
The next question you might
wondering is, how is it free?

00:23:41.438 --> 00:23:43.450
'Cause it costs many millions
of dollars ever year,

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:46.000
many tens of millions of
dollars every year to put out,

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:49.910
even to start server costs
are $7 million a year,

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:51.310
and I have a feeling based on some

00:23:51.310 --> 00:23:52.860
of the numbers we saw yesterday

00:23:53.704 --> 00:23:55.355
that they're going to be in excess

00:23:55.355 --> 00:23:57.246
of $10 million a year shortly.

00:23:57.246 --> 00:23:59.103
That's paid for by
philanthropic donations.

00:23:59.103 --> 00:24:01.270
And so I will say if any of you,

00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:03.240
for those of you who are
in a position to donate,

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:06.060
you can go to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.290
If you find value from Khan Academy,

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:10.630
if you want to ensure
that it's up and running

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:12.310
as we go through this crisis that students

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:14.560
around the world who
don't have the resources

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:17.940
or don't have access to
high quality materials,

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:22.363
think about even a $5, $10,
one-time or monthly donation.

00:24:22.363 --> 00:24:27.023
But that's how we are free for everyone.

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:31.313
Let's see, this is from Dishonest Abe,

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:37.363
I respect your honesty in your username.

00:24:38.561 --> 00:24:42.960
Where do you get the emotional
energy to learn all the time?

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:45.960
You know, for me,
learning actually gives me

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:49.310
emotional energy, I tell a lot of folks

00:24:49.310 --> 00:24:51.020
when they learn that Khan Academy

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:53.343
is now a 200-person-plus organization,

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:57.080
they kind of imagine that
I'm your traditional CEO

00:24:57.080 --> 00:24:59.260
who might sit in a boardroom all day

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:00.920
and be in meetings all day,

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:04.550
and some of that is kind of true,

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:07.330
but I'm a fairly nontraditional CEO.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:10.030
But for me, and all of
that's really important,

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:12.200
you have to have a team
that you get aligned,

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:15.700
that you can work with, that
you can do big things together,

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:17.829
because you're not gonna be
able to do it by yourself.

00:25:17.829 --> 00:25:18.920
And this is true even beyond Khan Academy,

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:21.220
why we form partnerships
and things like that.

00:25:22.434 --> 00:25:23.730
But for my emotional energy,

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:25.500
for me to be able to get
into the walk-in closet

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:26.910
or go into my office and to be able

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:30.960
to learn some new history or new science

00:25:32.214 --> 00:25:33.580
or just to be able to
communicate something

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:36.644
that I've always loved,
which is almost everything

00:25:36.644 --> 00:25:38.613
I ever learned, that's the stuff

00:25:38.613 --> 00:25:39.450
that puts me in a good mood.

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:41.623
When I come home and I'm grumpy,

00:25:42.875 --> 00:25:44.510
my wife will say, you probably
didn't make any videos today

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.450
and she's usually accurate.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:48.450
I'm usually in a good mode
once I've had a chance

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:51.670
to immerse myself in
some academic content.

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:53.800
And I think the thing
you have to appreciate

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.290
is this content that
you're seeing in textbooks

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:59.330
or Khan Academy, almost every concept

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:02.630
someone had dedicated
their life to figuring out.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:04.570
And sometimes people dedicate
their lives to figure out

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:06.304
and they couldn't figure it out.

00:26:06.304 --> 00:26:07.990
They couldn't figure
out, how did plants grow,

00:26:08.893 --> 00:26:10.150
how did they take carbon from the air

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:13.530
and use sunlight and water
to somehow store energy?

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:16.730
How do we know that A
squared plus B squared

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:17.730
is equal to C squared?

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:20.160
People pondered this
for sometimes decades,

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:22.780
hundreds of years, and
you have the answers.

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:24.280
And it's all there, and you just have

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:25.320
to decode it a little bit.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:27.632
It's like you're Harry, and you're like,

00:26:27.632 --> 00:26:29.378
wow, someone figured out all this stuff

00:26:29.378 --> 00:26:31.460
and I can learn this magic without having

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.160
to go through all of
that and maybe one day

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:34.030
push the frontier.

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:38.673
So Dishonest Abe, I think it's just

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:41.030
if you look at it the right way,

00:26:41.030 --> 00:26:43.570
it's incredibly magical and it could be

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:45.630
a source of emotional energy, of learning,

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:48.480
versus a drain on emotional energy.

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:53.480
So let's see, maybe we have
time for one more question.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:57.500
And let's see, the
science enthusiast says,

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:00.160
my sister is struggling with fractions,

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:01.920
I want to teach her.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:05.440
How can I teach her in
a way that she likes?

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:07.930
So I think the number one thing,

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:09.900
so if she's struggling with fractions,

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:12.200
I would go to Khan Academy's
section on fractions,

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:13.660
you can search on our page.

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:15.260
You can even actually go to arithmetic,

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:16.960
the whole arithmetic
course on Khan Academy,

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:18.800
there's a whole unit on fractions.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:20.840
And I would start at the
beginning of that unit,

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:22.960
and I would have her work on that material

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:24.080
while you are sitting next to her.

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:25.720
Don't answer the questions for her.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.220
You can give her little
pushes every now and then,

00:27:28.220 --> 00:27:30.540
little nudges if she's
getting a little demotivated

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:32.220
or she needs a little bit of a reminder,

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:34.807
or you just need to say,
well, read the question again,

00:27:34.807 --> 00:27:36.186
what are they really asking?

00:27:36.186 --> 00:27:37.609
And see how she's doing.

00:27:37.609 --> 00:27:38.942
If you think her question,

00:27:38.942 --> 00:27:40.040
her problem is really in fractions,

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.840
then try to just answer any
questions she might have,

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:44.470
but always push her,
if she has a question,

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:46.367
say watch the video first.

00:27:46.367 --> 00:27:49.070
Let her build that sense of agency

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:51.420
so that she can learn to learn

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:53.440
and you're there to support her.

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:56.440
But if the video isn't
properly explaining it

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:57.760
or it's just not resonating with her,

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:00.340
the hints to each of the exercise items

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:03.020
are not resonating with her,
then it's a great for saying,

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:04.700
okay, well let's think about it like this.

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:06.710
But I think the hardest thing
when you're doing it live

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:09.690
is the patience, and I say
that because people say

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:11.924
I sound very patient in
the videos, hopefully I do,

00:28:11.924 --> 00:28:14.909
but sometimes when you're,

00:28:14.909 --> 00:28:17.368
when I'm with my kids sometimes

00:28:17.368 --> 00:28:21.680
and I feel like I'm, I think
their brain is drifting,

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:23.330
they're probably getting
frustrated with me

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:25.190
because they're thinking
dad's saying the same thing

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:27.990
over and over again and
it's not making progress.

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:31.440
So respect, appreciate that she is trying,

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:33.670
that she's not just drifting
and she's not listening to you

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:35.570
and just try to be as patient as possible.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:38.400
I think that's where tutoring
session oftentimes break down.

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:41.260
The tutor gets frustrated,
the student gets frustrated,

00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:43.320
and everyone wants to walk away.

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:45.700
So have that patience,
keep motivating her,

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:47.590
and I think little by
little, I saw this Nadia

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:50.690
back in the day in 2004
when I was tutoring her,

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:52.760
as long as you keep that regularity

00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:54.950
I think you are going to
see a lot of progress.

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.160
If you see that even at the
earliest fractions thing,

00:28:57.160 --> 00:29:01.271
so even adding 2/3 to 1/3
somehow she has some difficulties

00:29:01.271 --> 00:29:03.930
with things like that,
it might be from gaps

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.610
that are coming earlier
in her learning journey

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:09.480
in some of the more basic arithmetic.

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:11.810
So that's another thing
that you might want to do

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:15.130
is have her start earlier
and build some confidence.

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.430
That's one thing we've been
recommending on the schedules

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:19.420
is having students maybe even start

00:29:19.420 --> 00:29:20.670
as early as kindergarten.

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:22.850
For example, your sister
I'm guessing she's a third

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:25.620
or a fourth grade student,
start her on kindergarten,

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:27.310
and she'll get through that super fast.

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:29.010
She might learn a thing or two,

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:30.540
probably will take her half an hour,

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:31.840
an hour to get through kindergarten,

00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:33.590
then first grade might take her an hour.

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.323
It'll build confidence.

00:29:35.323 --> 00:29:36.410
She'll see that, okay, she can do this,

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:37.840
then second grade, then third grade.

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:39.070
If she gets to third grade, she's going

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:42.330
to start hitting her zone
of proximal development,

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:43.953
her learning edge, so to speak,

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.500
but she'll know that she's approaching it

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:48.608
with a very strong foundation.

00:29:48.608 --> 00:29:50.570
And the more that you can
be around there for her,

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:52.690
the better, and motivate her.

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:55.500
So I will, I think that's all for today,

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.650
especially with all of
the technical difficulty.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:29:59.870
Thanks everyone for being part of this.

00:29:59.870 --> 00:30:04.180
This is one of the things
that keeps me happy

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:07.040
in this time of what
we call here in the US

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:10.050
social distancing to
hopefully stop the spread

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:13.280
of this virus, but I
really enjoy connecting

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:14.113
with all of you.

00:30:14.113 --> 00:30:16.500
I hope that this provides
some form of connection

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:19.890
in this time of social distancing
for all of you as well.

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:22.734
And stay tuned, I'm
gonna do this every day

00:30:22.734 --> 00:30:25.570
so that we feel together,
we want to support you

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:27.070
not only on your learning journeys

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:30.090
but just as human beings,
this is something,

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.240
one day someone will
do Khan Academy videos

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:34.580
about this time in history, 'cause it's,

00:30:34.580 --> 00:30:39.510
I think it's pretty interesting.

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:42.003
So I will leave y'all,
and I'll see you tomorrow.

